<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Johannes Käbin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1905">1905</a>–<a href="/years/1999">1999</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Ivan Kabin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Johannes Käbin was an Estonian communist, raised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, who served as First Secretary of the Communist Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a> from 1950 to 1978. He led Soviet Estonia through the post-Stalin decades of industrialisation, oil shale development and large-scale Russian-speaking immigration, while allowing some room for Estonian culture. He later served as chairman of the republic's Supreme Soviet presidium.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/johannes-kabin.jpg" alt="Johannes Käbin" width="320" height="397" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jaan Künnap · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Johannes_K%C3%A4bin_1978.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
